  "worked_solution": "The polar curve $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ is a circle of radius $\\frac{23}{6}$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis where $r=\\frac{23}{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959689/assets/6cab0604a3a8a45de244519cd407d310e8add3978e13cd20e1ef6b3d38e191fd.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(\\,\\frac{23}{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,).$",
